ed Nu free rein in all areas not directly affecting the army."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_b8e1fcd54ac2c3df7f63","corpus":"cib","date":"1960-11-18","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A005400160001-4","earlier_page":11,"end":859,"page_sha256":"726817b11da31506bc240aeb3ad3aa9962969fbc16f57712acbc015949208cc9","start":813,"text":"the deterioration of government administration"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_b8e1fcd54ac2c3df7f63","corpus":"cib","date":"1960-11-18","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A005400160001-4","earlier_page":11,"end":1330,"page_sha256":"726817b11da31506bc240aeb3ad3aa9962969fbc16f57712acbc015949208cc9","start":535,"text":"General Ne Win is reluctant to intervene, as he fears the corrupting influence of political power on the officer corps. He overrode army objections to permit the February national elections and U Nu's return as prime minister. Although he is reportedly seriously concerned with the deterioration of government administration since he left office, he has permitted Nu free rein in all areas not directly affecting the army. The flare-up with Maung Maung reportedly occurred at a 10 November meeting at which Ne Win insisted that the army should avoid any action which might be offensive to the Chinese Communists or to Burma's civilian political leaders. Brigadier Aung Gyi, the third member of the army top command, although present at the discussion, is not reported to have taken any position."},"id":"C3"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1960-11-18","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A005400160001-4","earlier_page":11,"id":"S1","page_sha256":"726817b11da31506bc240aeb3ad3aa9962969fbc16f57712acbc015949208cc9"},{"canonical_page_id":"page_1846a145ffa231f6c109","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_1846a145ffa231f6c109","corpus":"cib","date":"1958-09-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004000150001-0","earlier_page":9,"end":1057,"page_sha256":"bad21f2a0bc88f5b58383d81619cde09eb5746512b23c08befff7903ab75ad37","start":838,"text":"The army, even before the June government crisis, was critical of Nu's generosity to surrendered insurgents and fearful that Communists would use cached arms to renew violence either before or during national elections."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_1846a145ffa231f6c109","corpus":"cib","date":"1958-09-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004000150001-0","earlier_page":9,"end":945,"page_sha256":"bad21f2a0bc88f5b58383d81619cde09eb5746512b23c08befff7903ab75ad37","start":904,"text":"Nu's generosity to surrendered insurgents"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_1846a145ffa231f6c109","corpus":"cib","date":"1958-09-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004000150001-0","earlier_page":9,"end":1199,"page_sha256":"bad21f2a0bc88f5b58383d81619cde09eb5746512b23c08befff7903ab75ad37","start":710,"text":"The members of the People's Comrade party who had surrendered recently are already leaving Rangoon and returning to the jungle. The army, even before the June government crisis, was critical of Nu's generosity to surrendered insurgents and fearful that Communists would use cached arms to renew violence either before or during national elections. Though Ne Win is pledged to continue general government policies, terms for insurgent surrenders are expected to be made much more stringent."},"id":"C3"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1958-09-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004000150001-0","earlier_page":9,"id":"S2","page_sha256":"bad21f2a0bc88f5b58383d81619cde09eb5746512b23c08befff7903ab75ad37"}],"used_for_inference":false,"version":"historical-recovery-study-digest-input/1"}